---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import DeployButton from '../components/DeployButton.astro';
import snippets from '../../../../docs/onboarding/snippets.json';
import {installShell,installNpm,installSdk,githubUrl,defaultBranch} from '../lib/links.mjs';
const sections=[{id:'deploy',label:'Deploy'},{id:'install',label:'Install & connect'},{id:'capture',label:'Capture'},{id:'sync',label:'Sync'},{id:'agent',label:'Agent'},{id:'report',label:'Report'}];
---
<Page title="Quickstart" description="Deploy your own Slurp Worker on Cloudflare, connect the CLI, capture events, sync to DuckDB, and give your agent local data." current="docs" sections={sections}>
<header style="padding-bottom:40px"><p class="eyebrow">Quickstart</p><h1>From your app<br/><span class="hot">to your agent.</span></h1><p class="lede">Deploy a Worker to your own Cloudflare account, connect the CLI, and keep your events in one local database. Install the CLI with one command, on macOS or Linux.</p></header>
<section class="section" id="deploy"><span class="section-number">00 / DEPLOY</span><h2>Put the Worker in your account.</h2><p>One Worker, two Durable Object classes and an R2 bucket, deployed by <code>slurp cloud init</code>. You need a Cloudflare account (the Free plan is enough to start) and Node 20+. The dialog has the commands.</p><div class="actions"><DeployButton id="docs"/><a class="btn" href="/self-host/">Cost and limits</a></div></section>
<section class="section" id="install"><span class="section-number">01 / INSTALL & CONNECT</span><h2>Make a home for your events.</h2><p>Install the CLI with the shell command or npm. <code>slurp cloud init</code> deploys your Worker and connects this computer to it. Already have one? <code>slurp connect &lt;url&gt; --token-stdin</code> checks its admin token and remembers it. Setup finds DuckDB or offers to install it. Init creates or selects an app by name. Run <code>slurp projects</code> to list registered projects.</p><Code label="Terminal" track="install_copied" code={`${installShell}
# or: ${installNpm}
slurp cloud init
slurp setup
cd /path/to/your/app
slurp init --name "My app"`}/><p><code>slurp init</code> opens a private offline quickstart with your project’s SDK instructions. Rerun init to refresh that page.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="capture"><span class="section-number">02 / CAPTURE</span><h2>Send your first event.</h2><p>Install the SDK from npm, then paste the snippet <code>slurp init</code> shows for your app: it has your real ingest URL, source ID and publishable key. Publishable keys are ingest-only, so they are safe in a web page.</p><Code label="Terminal" code={installSdk}/><Code label="JavaScript / Node" code={snippets.sdk}/><p>User feedback is just another event: <code>{"track('feedback', { message, rating });"}</code> Your agent finds it later with SQL.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Test from your terminal.</h2><p>Inside your app or a subfolder, Slurp uses the nearest project binding and its saved SDK key.</p><Code label="Your app folder" code={`slurp send signed_up --user-id demo_alice --data '{"demo":true}'
slurp sync`}/><p class="fine">Sending confirms durable acceptance. Events appear in <code>slurp sync</code> after your Worker next seals an archive, about every 10 minutes by default (earlier when a batch reaches 8 MB).</p></section>
<section class="section" id="http"><h2>Send events over HTTP.</h2><p>Any language can POST canonical events as newline-delimited JSON to <code>/v1/events</code> on your Worker. Use <code>Authorization: Bearer YOUR_PUBLISHABLE_KEY</code> and <code>Content-Type: application/x-ndjson</code>. Tools that send webhooks (GitHub, Stripe, anything) use <a href="#webhooks">their own endpoint</a>.</p><Code label="One NDJSON record (end with a newline)" code={'{"id":"YOUR_UNIQUE_EVENT_ID","type":"track","timestamp":"2026-11-05T12:00:00Z","event":"signed_up","data":{"plan":"pro"}}'}/><p class="fine">A successful response means durable acceptance. Retrying the exact same bytes is safe: the Worker answers with <code>Slurp-Duplicate: 1</code> and local import ignores repeated event IDs.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="webhooks"><h2>Webhooks.</h2><p>Point GitHub, Stripe or any tool that can POST at your Worker. Each gets its own named key, so its events carry <code>sender = 'github'</code> and the payload is stored whole as the event's data. Releases and deployments show up in a <code>releases</code> view you can join to your product events.</p><Code label="Terminal · prints the URL, the secret and the steps" code={'slurp project webhook PROJECT github\nslurp project webhook PROJECT stripe --secret whsec_…\nslurp project webhook PROJECT generic'}/><p class="fine">Preview: tested against the providers\' documented signature examples, not yet live deliveries. Stripe chooses its own signing secret, so you give it to Slurp. Details: <a href="https://github.com/hitSlop/slurp/blob/master/docs/webhooks.md">webhooks.md</a>.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="sync"><span class="section-number">03 / SYNC</span><h2>Bring the events home.</h2><Code label="Terminal · your workspace" code={snippets.sync}/><p>Run <code>slurp sync</code> to download new archives from your Worker into your local DuckDB database. Recently accepted events may still be waiting for the next seal. Close open DuckDB sessions before syncing.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="agent"><span class="section-number">04 / AGENT</span><h2>Give your agent the context.</h2><Code label="Install the skill bundle" code="slurp agent setup --out .agents/skills/slurp --all"/><p>Ask your local coding agent to inspect the workspace and query its events. For a bot running on another machine, create a separate revocable read key:</p><Code label="Create bot access" code={snippets.key}/><p>Transfer the returned key through the bot’s secret store as <code>SLURP_TOKEN</code>, then run these steps on its machine:</p><Code label="Bot machine" code={snippets.agent}/><p class="fine">Keep keys out of URLs, reports, and skill files. Revoke a key with <code>slurp project key revoke</code>.</p><h3>Or connect an AI client over MCP</h3><p><code>slurp mcp</code> lets Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Claude Desktop or Windsurf read your events directly: describe the schema, run read-only SQL (in a sandbox that cannot read local files), sync and build reports. Sending events is off unless you add <code>--allow-send</code>.</p><Code label="Add the MCP server" code={snippets.mcp}/><h3>Post reports to Slack or Discord</h3><Code label="Scheduled report" code={snippets.notify}/><p class="fine">Setup for every client, the tools, the sandbox and a cron recipe: <a href={`${githubUrl}/blob/${defaultBranch}/docs/integrations.md`}>docs/integrations.md</a>.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="report"><span class="section-number">05 / REPORT</span><h2>Keep the query with the finding.</h2><p>Ask your agent to create <code>report.md</code> using the repository’s <code>examples/events.md</code> as a reference. Reports combine Markdown, named SQL queries, and metric, table, or chart blocks.</p><Code label="Validate, build, open" code={`slurp report check report.md
slurp report build report.md --out ./report --png --pdf
slurp report open ./report`}/><p>HTML embeds charts and fonts. PNG and PDF are ready to share. Review the included data before sharing a report.</p></section>
</Page>
